class TypeError_(Exception): pass

def check(node, scope):
    if isinstance(node, Add):
        lt = check(node.left, scope)
        rt = check(node.right, scope)
        if lt == "string" and rt == "int":
            raise TypeError_(f"cannot add string and int: {node}")
        return lt  # assume matching numeric types otherwise
    if isinstance(node, Literal):
        return "string" if isinstance(node.value, str) else "int"
    if isinstance(node, Name):
        if node.name not in scope:
            raise NameError(f"undeclared variable: {node.name}")
        return scope[node.name]
```

This is a toy version of the exact check that rejects `"a" + 3`: `check` recurses into both operands of `Add`, gets back `"string"` and `"int"`, and the explicit rule for that combination raises. This runs *after* parsing succeeds — the tree in front of it is already known to be structurally valid; this pass is purely about meaning.
